`requestDiagnosticReport` uses `withTimeout(connection.sendRequest(...), DIAGNOSTICS_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS)` but `withTimeout` is implemented as a Promise.race that does not cancel the underlying JSON-RPC request. In `waitForDiagnostics` this can lead to multiple in-flight `textDocument/diagnostic` requests that are still pending on the server after the client has already timed out, potentially increasing server load and causing late/stale responses. Consider gating diagnostic polling on server support (e.g., `initialized.capabilities.diagnosticProvider` / registration) and/or implementing request cancellation (`$/cancelRequest`) or a single long-lived request instead of repeated timeouts.
```suggestion
  const inFlightDiagnosticRequests = new Map<string, Promise<DocumentDiagnosticReport | null>>()

  function getDiagnosticRequestKey(filePath: string, identifier?: string) {
    return `${filePath}::${identifier ?? ""}`
  }

  async function requestDiagnosticReport(filePath: string, identifier?: string) {
    const requestKey = getDiagnosticRequestKey(filePath, identifier)
    let request = inFlightDiagnosticRequests.get(requestKey)
    if (!request) {
      request = connection
        .sendRequest<DocumentDiagnosticReport | null>("textDocument/diagnostic", {
          ...(identifier ? { identifier } : {}),
          textDocument: {
            uri: pathToFileURL(filePath).href,
          },
        })
        .finally(() => {
          if (inFlightDiagnosticRequests.get(requestKey) === request) {
            inFlightDiagnosticRequests.delete(requestKey)
          }
        })
      inFlightDiagnosticRequests.set(requestKey, request)
    }

    const report = await withTimeout(request, DIAGNOSTICS_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS).catch(() => null)
```

waitForDiagnostics() chooses the overall timeout based on `diagnosticRegistrations.size` at call time, but that map can be updated later via `client/registerCapability`. If a server registers pull diagnostics after waitForDiagnostics() starts (or takes longer than 3s to compute the first pull result), the outer withTimeout can fire too early even though pull diagnostics are available. Consider computing the timeout more defensively (e.g., always using the pull timeout when pull diagnostics are supported/advertised, or extending the timeout once registrations appear / once a pull request is in-flight).
```suggestion
      // Dynamic diagnostic registrations can appear after this method starts,
      // so choose a conservative timeout that remains valid if pull diagnostics
      // become available later or take longer to produce an initial result.
      const timeout = Math.max(DIAGNOSTICS_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS, DIAGNOSTICS_PULL_WAIT_TIMEOUT_MS)
```

