`deriveNewContentsFromChunks` reads the original file via `readFileSync(filePath, "utf-8")` and then calls `Bom.split(...)`. In Node, reading as a UTF-8 string commonly strips the BOM, which would make `originalContent.bom` incorrect and can cause BOMs to be lost on subsequent writes. To make BOM detection reliable across runtimes, read bytes (`readFileSync(filePath)`) and decode with `TextDecoder("utf-8", { ignoreBOM: true })` (or otherwise ensure the BOM codepoint is preserved) before calling `Bom.split`.
```suggestion
    const originalBytes = readFileSync(filePath)
    const originalText = new TextDecoder("utf-8", { ignoreBOM: true }).decode(originalBytes)
    originalContent = Bom.split(originalText)
```

In the `oldString === ""` overwrite path, `desiredBom` is derived only from `params.newString` (`Bom.split(params.newString)`), so overwriting an existing BOM-prefixed file with BOM-less `newString` will drop the file’s BOM. Since you already compute `existed`, consider reading the existing file’s BOM (e.g., via `Bom.readFile`) and using `source.bom || next.bom` (similar to `WriteTool`) so overwrites preserve BOM by default.
```suggestion
                const source = existed ? yield* Bom.readFile(afs, filePath) : { bom: "", text: "" }
                const next = Bom.split(params.newString)
                const desiredBom = source.bom || next.bom
