`dotnet tool install --global` installs into the user's global tool location (e.g. ~/.dotnet/tools), but this function later assumes the binary will be in `Global.Path.bin`. That mismatch will make the auto-install path return a non-existent executable and prevent the server from spawning. Use `--tool-path ${Global.Path.bin}` (consistent with the fsautocomplete install below), or re-run `which("roslyn-language-server")` after install and return that result instead of constructing a `Global.Path.bin` path.
```suggestion
  const proc = Process.spawn(
    ["dotnet", "tool", "install", "--tool-path", Global.Path.bin, "roslyn-language-server", "--prerelease"],
    {
      stdout: "pipe",
      stderr: "pipe",
      stdin: "pipe",
    },
  )
```

After installing, we should verify the resolved `bin` actually exists (and ideally prefer resolving via `which()` again) before returning it. As-is, this can return a path that doesn't exist and later fail with a confusing spawn error.
```suggestion
  const resolved = which("roslyn-language-server")
  if (resolved && (await pathExists(resolved))) {
    log.info(`installed roslyn-language-server`, { bin: resolved })
    return resolved
  }

  const bin = path.join(Global.Path.bin, "roslyn-language-server" + (process.platform === "win32" ? ".exe" : ""))
  if (await pathExists(bin)) {
    log.info(`installed roslyn-language-server`, { bin })
    return bin
  }

  log.error("Installed roslyn-language-server but could not resolve executable", { resolved, bin })
  return
```

